azgor wrote:
> This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
> freenode.net.  Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.
>
> [1]: Spinach golem: The rarest monster in nethack; a living specimen
> has never been seen. Its corpse is sometimes encountered, but only in
> tins. -azgor

...and the suggestion here would be...?  Assuming the proposal is to
actually make such a beast exist, I'd say no.  Getting hit by spinach
doesn't hurt (unless it's frozen), so there'd be little to no risk to
the monster, with an obvious big gain from killing it...

> [2]: Each role should have a default name for his pets (as in Samurai
> => Hachi) -moussambani

Eh.  I already de-name my pets if they start out with a name (Samurai
and Cavemen, I believe), so I can tell how it's doing as it grows from
puppyhood.  Maybe if the various sizes of dog/cat/horse had different
colors, but I think we're already pretty maxed out on those...

> [3]: Tigers should occasionally drop tigereye on death. This should
> glow brightly for several hundred turns, and be chargable. (Tiger,
> Tiger, burning bright) -SpeedEvil

Again, eh.  It'd make more sense to slightly increase the possibility
of tigers dropping tiger eye rings, but a new item with no real-world
basis, and only a slight literary one?  No thanks.  Maybe a YAFM
related to zapping a tiger with a wand of fire, but that's as far as
I'd take it.

> [4]: When attacking a Jaguar with a pickaxe or mattock, the Jaguar
> should eat the weapon, and display the message "Oh no, that was a 2
> litre Jaguar". -SpeedEvil

As with others who've posted here, this makes no sense to me.  What's
the reference/pun?

> [5]: all felines should - when the player has a light, be visible as 'f
> - an unknown feline' at double the normal distance, due to their
> reflective eyes. -SpeedEvil

This would confuse anyone who wasn't privy to the idea's inception.
Perhaps, instead, polymorphing into a feline (or other critter with
great night vision but no infravision; would jaberwocks--"eyes of
flame"--qualify?) should give a +1 bonus to light radius?

> [6]: The Gazebo. A golem. It engulfs, but does no damage. Vulnerable to
> fire. -SpeedEvil

Oh, joy, another golem idea.  What's up with these?  And, why create
another monster that does no damage--aren't lichen easy enough for you?
 (Of course, if it has an engulf attack, wouldn't it be fatal to pets,
even though it does no damage?)

> [7]: Corpses of unique monsters could be worth much cash and points.
> -GreyKnight

I'm assuming you mean points at ascension.  What's the point?  Aren't
bragging rights in your YAAP for bringing along tins of everyone
enough?

> [8]: If player is wearing a ring of sustain ability while polymorphing
> into a creature which has lower stat(s) than the previous form had, the
> ring has a chance of exploding or flying in a random direction. -Zanasj

Eh.  Moving right along.

> [9]: Flesh golem corpses should be a bit like tins, before eating:
> "This smells like <random_monster> flesh. Eat it? [y/n]". Of course
> possibly giving appropriate intrinsics etc. -Duke-

So, you stone-to-flesh a couple boulders, and polymorph the resultant
chunks of meat, kill the resultant flesh golem, and it's suddenly made
of cockatrice/tengu/black dragon meat?  How's that supposed to work?

> [10]: Magic golem, made of various magical accoutrements (magical
> potions, scrolls, wands, spellbooks). Can breathe magic missiles and is
> resistant to magic. -GreyKnight

Gah!  Stop it with the golems already!  For one thing, golems don't
breathe, so there's no logic to giving them a breath attack.

> [11]: If a camera is destroyed with N charges or more remaining, a
> yellow light pops out of the remains. For bonus fun, produce a black
> light instead if the camera was cursed. -GreyKnight [N lights might be
> even more fun. -ed.]

Better would be to have an imp pop out, ideally with a
tame/peaceful/hostile status similar to applying figurines.  Read
Pratchett for justification.  And someone's already posted a link to a
patch along this line.

> [12]: new monster: albatross.  represented as white B.  arrows and
> crossbow bolts do double damage.  always leaves a corpse unless it came
> back via #turn undead.  taming one provides +3 luck while it's still
> your pet,  killing one is -3 luck.  Relatively rare in appearance,
> non-genocidable, non-figurinable.  Corpse can be worn as an amulet in
> the amulet slot; until it decays, it will act as a non-turning-to-dust
> AoLS.  While wearing the albatross corpse, your luck is fixed at -3.
> -lorimer

Is there *any* non-unique monster that's non-figurinable?  Luck bonuses
are fairly useless (I'm assuming the 14 cap still applies), and giving
someone a non-turning-to-dust AoLS is not worth considering.  Besides,
it'd be more appropriate for it to auto-curse and set your luck to -14
when worn.

> [13]: New monster: Brownie. A gnome like D&D character. Should be less
> powerful than a gnome and [inhabit] upper gnomish mines. -Gaulgath

Gnomes are already weak--why is there a need to create an even weaker
creature?

> [14]: When a boulder ... falls on an altar it should crush it. ie
> reading a scroll of earth on top of an altar. -Gaulgath

So, in the middle of a sac-fest, I lure a giant onto the altar, kill
it, and it drops its boulders--destroying the altar?  Ouch.  And I
suppose you're also going to suggest that kicking altars (especially as
a sasquatch/wearing kicking boots, etc.) should also have a chance to
destroy the altar?  No, thanks.  And that's ignoring the question of
what sort of penalty should apply, and when (does killing the
afore-mentioned giant trigger a penalty, for instance?)

> [15]: Attempting to #name a specific object, especially when trying
> things like the Helm of Brilliance identification trick, should break
> illiterate conduct --- the text of the game specifically says "Your
> hand slips while engraving" and the information stays around even if
> you get flayed. -raxvulpine

Until yesterday's discussion, I didn't even realize that #naming a
specific item was "engraving".  I thought it was "Hey, I'll call this
Bob!"  And what are we supposed to be engraving with, anyhow?  Why can
I engrave on my crystal plate mail (with no appropriate tools), but not
the dungeon floor?  And, I don't believe #names are preserved in bones
files, but if they're engravings, they should.  All that to say, I
think that #naming an object shouldn't break illiteracy, nor should you
be prevented from #naming an item after the artifact(s) of the same
type.  And, of course, #names should be subject to flayer attacks.

> [16]: Entering Delphi as a woodchuck should produce a YAFM. -HappyFoppy
> [What message? -ed.]

Yes, what message?  And what's the source of the Oracle/woodchuck
anyhow--I've never gotten the joke.

> [17]: Getting a towel wet should transform it into a wet towel, with a
> corresponding increase in weight and a devastating increase in damage.
> -Grunt

Isn't this one of the most frequently raised YANIs?

> [18]: More gourmet tin messages. "You consume terrine of Green-elf in a
> delicate black pudding sauce." "You consume salt-and-cardamom-cured
> kraken gravlax." -cjeris

Eh.

> [19]: Grant a bonus to effective Int+Cha for foocubus interaction if
> the player is wielding a bullwhip, carrying an iron chain, or both.
> -cjeris

Doesn't the rgrn FAQ say not to suggest this sort of thing, since it's
